{"name":"Tyrolite Blue-Green","hex":"#00a499","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"tyrolite","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hydrated calcium copper arsenate carbonate mineral that forms glassy blue to green orthorhombic radial crystals and botryoidal masses."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tyrolite"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tyrolite","blocks":[{"text":"Tyrolite is a hydrous calcium copper arsenate carbonate mineral with the formula CaCu5(AsO4)2CO3(OH)4⋅6H2O. Tyrolite forms glassy, blue to green orthorhombic radial crystals and botryoidal masses. It has a Mohs hardness of 1.5–2.0 and a specific gravity of 3.1–3.2. It is translucent with refractive indices of nα = 1.694, nβ = 1.726, and nγ = 1.730."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Tyrolite-256893.jpg/330px-Tyrolite-256893.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tyrolite"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Chrysocolla-Tyrolite-Clinotyrolite-202108.jpg/500px-Chrysocolla-Tyrolite-Clinotyrolite-202108.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Chrysocolla-Tyrolite-Clinotyrolite-202108.jpg/500px-Chrysocolla-Tyrolite-Clinotyrolite-202108.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Chrysocolla on tyrolite and clinotyrolite specimen (4.0 cm × 2.6 cm × 2.6 cm (1.6 in × 1.0 in × 1.0 in)); from San Simon Mine in Santa Rosa-Huantajaya District, Iquique Province, Chile.(Click image for details of the mineral chemistry.)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChrysocolla-Tyrolite-Clinotyrolite-202108.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/TYROLITE%2C_AZURITE_FROM_ZAPATCHITZA_DEPOSIT%2C_WESTERN_STARA_PLANINA_-_COLL._Y._MINTCHEVA-STEFANOVA_AT_SU_%27ST._KLIMENT_OHRIDSKI%27_MUSEUM_OF_MINERALOGY.jpg/500px-TYROLITE%2C_AZURITE_FROM_ZAPATCHITZA_DEPOSIT%2C_WESTERN_STARA_PLANINA_-_COLL._Y._MINTCHEVA-STEFANOVA_AT_SU_%27ST._KLIMENT_OHRIDSKI%27_MUSEUM_OF_MINERALOGY.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/TYROLITE%2C_AZURITE_FROM_ZAPATCHITZA_DEPOSIT%2C_WESTERN_STARA_PLANINA_-_COLL._Y._MINTCHEVA-STEFANOVA_AT_SU_%27ST._KLIMENT_OHRIDSKI%27_MUSEUM_OF_MINERALOGY.jpg/1280px-TYROLITE%2C_AZURITE_FROM_ZAPATCHITZA_DEPOSIT%2C_WESTERN_STARA_PLANINA_-_COLL._Y._MINTCHEVA-STEFANOVA_AT_SU_%27ST._KLIMENT_OHRIDSKI%27_MUSEUM_OF_MINERALOGY.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/TYROLITE%2C_AZURITE_FROM_ZAPATCHITZA_DEPOSIT%2C_WESTERN_STARA_PLANINA_-_COLL._Y._MINTCHEVA-STEFANOVA_AT_SU_%27ST._KLIMENT_OHRIDSKI%27_MUSEUM_OF_MINERALOGY.jpg/500px-TYROLITE%2C_AZURITE_FROM_ZAPATCHITZA_DEPOSIT%2C_WESTERN_STARA_PLANINA_-_COLL._Y._MINTCHEVA-STEFANOVA_AT_SU_%27ST._KLIMENT_OHRIDSKI%27_MUSEUM_OF_MINERALOGY.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_c